### What does it mean for a binary tree to be balanced?

A binary tree is height-balanced when, at every single node, the heights of its left and right subtrees differ by at most one. This property ensures that the tree does not degenerate into a long chain, which would make operations like search degrade from O(log n) to O(n).

```java
package com.bank.model;

public class AccountIndexNode {
    public String accountNumber;
    public AccountIndexNode left;
    public AccountIndexNode right;

    public AccountIndexNode(String accountNumber) {
        this.accountNumber = accountNumber;
    }
}
```

### How does the efficient fused approach work?

Write a helper that returns the height of a subtree, or -1 if any part of it is unbalanced. Recurse left, check for sentinel, recurse right, check for sentinel, compare heights, and return.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.AccountIndexNode;

public class BalanceChecker {
    public static boolean isBalanced(AccountIndexNode root) {
        return checkHeight(root) != -1;
    }

    private static int checkHeight(AccountIndexNode node) {
        if (node == null) return 0;

        int left = checkHeight(node.left);
        if (left == -1) return -1; // short-circuit

        int right = checkHeight(node.right);
        if (right == -1) return -1; // short-circuit

        if (Math.abs(left - right) > 1) return -1; // imbalanced here

        return 1 + Math.max(left, right); // height of this subtree
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        //     ACC-100
        //    /       \
        // ACC-050   ACC-150
        //   /
        // ACC-025
        //   /
        // ACC-010
        AccountIndexNode root = new AccountIndexNode("ACC-100");
        root.left = new AccountIndexNode("ACC-050");
        root.right = new AccountIndexNode("ACC-150");
        root.left.left = new AccountIndexNode("ACC-025");
        root.left.left.left = new AccountIndexNode("ACC-010");

        System.out.println("Is balanced: " + isBalanced(root));
    }
}
```

```text
Is balanced: false
```

### Why is the naive approach quadratic?

The naive approach computes the height of every node's subtrees with a separate recursion and checks the difference. This recomputes heights repeatedly. On a skewed tree (n nodes in a straight line), the height computation at the root is O(n), at its child O(n-1), and so on, giving O(n^2) total.

```java
// NAIVE: O(n*h) time, quadratic on skewed trees
public static boolean isBalancedNaive(AccountIndexNode node) {
    if (node == null) return true;
    int leftH = height(node.left);
    int rightH = height(node.right);
    return Math.abs(leftH - rightH) <= 1
        && isBalancedNaive(node.left)    // recomputes heights below
        && isBalancedNaive(node.right);  // recomputes heights below
}

private static int height(AccountIndexNode node) {
    if (node == null) return 0;
    return 1 + Math.max(height(node.left), height(node.right));
}
```

### How does the sentinel short-circuit work?

Once any subtree is found unbalanced, the helper returns -1 immediately. The parent checks for -1 before recursing into the other child, so no further height computation happens. This early exit is what keeps the fused approach linear.

```java
// Short-circuit flow:
int left = checkHeight(node.left);
if (left == -1) return -1;  // stop immediately, do not recurse right

int right = checkHeight(node.right);
if (right == -1) return -1; // stop immediately, do not check abs diff
```

### What are the edge cases?

Empty tree (balanced), single node (balanced), perfectly balanced tree, and a tree that is balanced at the root but unbalanced deeper.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.AccountIndexNode;

public class BalanceEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Empty
        System.out.println("Empty: " + BalanceChecker.isBalanced(null));

        // Single
        System.out.println("Single: " + BalanceChecker.isBalanced(
            new AccountIndexNode("ACC-100")));

        // Balanced
        AccountIndexNode bal = new AccountIndexNode("ACC-100");
        bal.left = new AccountIndexNode("ACC-050");
        bal.right = new AccountIndexNode("ACC-150");
        System.out.println("Balanced: " + BalanceChecker.isBalanced(bal));
    }
}
```

```text
Empty: true
Single: true
Balanced: true
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

| Approach | Time | Space |
|---|---|---|
| Naive (recompute heights) | O(n * h), quadratic on skew | O(h) |
| Fused with sentinel | O(n) | O(h) |

The fused approach visits each node at most once and the sentinel short-circuit means it can exit even earlier in practice.

### How does this relate to AVL trees and red-black trees?

AVL trees and red-black trees are self-balancing binary search trees that maintain balance after every insertion and deletion. The balance check here is the same condition that AVL trees enforce (height difference at most 1 at every node). Red-black trees use a weaker balance condition that allows height differences up to 2x but is cheaper to maintain.

```java
// AVL rotation happens when |leftHeight - rightHeight| > 1
// The balance check in this problem is the same test
if (Math.abs(left - right) > 1) {
    // AVL tree would rotate here to restore balance
    // Our checker just returns -1 (unbalanced)
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should explain why the fused approach is linear while the naive approach is quadratic, and point to the short-circuit as the mechanism that makes it efficient. The fused post-order pattern, computing height while checking a property, appears in many tree problems.

In production at National Bank of India, the account-number index is a binary search tree. After a batch of inserts, a health check confirms the index is still balanced. If not, the service schedules a rebalance job because a lopsided index drives lookups toward linear time.

```java
// Production health check
if (!BalanceChecker.isBalanced(accountIndexRoot)) {
    scheduler.submit(new RebalanceJob(accountIndexRoot));
    alertService.send("Account index is unbalanced, rebalance scheduled.");
}
```
